<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- W3C SCXML 3.13 defines a macrostep as a chain of microsteps that ends in a
     configuration where nothing is enabled by NULL AND the internal event
     queue is empty. Appendix D's Principles and Constraints then say, in as
     many words, that the chain need not exist:

       "A microstep always terminates. A macrostep may not. A macrostep that
        does not terminate may be said to consist of an infinitely long
        sequence of microsteps. This is currently allowed."

     `eventless_macrostep_is_bounded` owns the half of that sentence built from
     transitions that need no event at all. This one owns the other half: a
     `<raise>` answered by a transition that raises again. Both are the same
     clause and the same allowance; they are different code in every engine
     here, because Appendix D's inner loop has two branches and only one of
     them had a ceiling.

     Measured 2026-08-20, before this fixture existed, the seven engines
     answered a two-state raise chain in two shapes and neither is one a host
     can act on:

       Rust, Go, Python, C11, C++ Interpreter, C++ AOT
                 did not return at all. The internal drain had no ceiling in
                 any of them: `step()` on a document whose transition raises
                 the event it answers is a core at 100% forever.
       Kotlin    stopped at a hundred and said nothing — no counter, no log
                 line, no return value. Bounded and silent is the same signal
                 as unbounded to the host reading it.

     Outcomes this document separates:

       poke      one ordinary transition       control: proves a run fired
       bounded   a chain that STOPS by itself  a thousand microsteps, exactly
                                               the ceiling, then the guard
                                               stops matching: the count
                                               stays 0
       spin      a chain that cannot stop      the engine stops taking the
                                               chain and says so
       resume    a chain LONGER than one       the refused links were left on
                 macrostep but finite          the queue: a second macrostep
                                               finishes them and counts nothing
       alternate one raise, one eventless      the case a per-branch budget
                 transition, forever           lets through: neither branch
                                               reaches the ceiling on its own

     `alternate` is why the budget is one number rather than two. An engine
     that gives the eventless branch its own thousand and the internal branch
     another never refuses this document at all: each branch keeps finding its
     own counter unspent, because the other branch is what is advancing. One
     of the seven shipped exactly that pair of counters. The witness is
     arithmetic — `alts` must be five hundred, half of the shared thousand, and
     not a thousand.

     `bounded` is the half that makes the count mean something, and its chain
     is deliberately exactly a thousand microsteps rather than three: the
     ceiling is on microsteps TAKEN, so a chain that ends at the ceiling is the
     case an off-by-one gets wrong. The sibling fixture measured two engines
     doing so, and in one of them the verdict called `stop()`.

     `resume` is the half that says what a refusal DID with the events it would
     not run. The engine leaves them queued, so the chain continues from where
     it was cut rather than dying there — the same document finishes on the
     second macrostep, and nothing is counted for it because that macrostep
     ended the way the clause says. An engine that dropped the queue would pass
     every other outcome here and fail this one.

     Every state here is entered and never left, and every chain is built from
     TARGETLESS transitions. That is not economy: the state a truncation names
     has to be one all seven engines can agree on. The C11 profile keeps its
     configuration in a bitmap and has no "current state" scalar to report, so
     it names the source of the transition the drain last took; the other six
     name the state the drain was standing in. On a chain that never leaves one
     state those are the same state, and the contract — "a state on the chain
     that could not settle" — is testable without asking the backends to agree
     on something they do not represent.

     There is no way back to `idle`, and each outcome gets its own machine the
     way the sibling fixtures' do. §scxml-3.13 gives internal events priority
     over external ones, so a document whose internal queue never empties would
     hold any rescuing event behind a chain that will not end — the engine
     reaches the external queue after a refusal precisely so that a machine
     stopped this way stays reachable at all.

     Deliberately single-axis: no `<send>`, no `<invoke>`, no `<parallel>`, and
     no failing expression — `error_cascade_is_bounded` owns the chain built
     from errors, `eventless_macrostep_is_bounded` owns the one built from
     transitions that need no event, and this one owns the chain built from
     `<raise>`.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0" datamodel="ecmascript" initial="idle"
       name="internal_chain_is_bounded">
    <datamodel>
        <!-- Written by the control transition only. A host that reads a
             counter of zero cannot tell "the run never happened" from "the
             run happened and did nothing", and every assertion below rests on
             telling those apart. -->
        <data id="pokes" expr="0"/>
        <!-- Links of the bounded chain. It stops itself at a thousand, which
             is exactly the ceiling. -->
        <data id="laps" expr="0"/>
        <!-- Links of the unbounded chain. Nothing in the document stops it. -->
        <data id="links" expr="0"/>
        <!-- Links of the chain that outlasts one macrostep and then ends: half
             again as long as the ceiling, so it takes two macrosteps and the
             second one completes. -->
        <data id="beats" expr="0"/>
        <!-- Links of the alternating chain, counted on the internal half only:
             one lap is one raise plus one eventless transition, so a shared
             budget of a thousand microsteps buys five hundred of them. -->
        <data id="alts" expr="0"/>
        <!-- The alternating chain's baton. `1` means the eventless half is
             enabled; the internal half sets it, the eventless half clears it
             and raises the next link. -->
        <data id="pending" expr="0"/>
    </datamodel>

    <state id="idle">
        <transition event="poke" target="idle">
            <assign location="pokes" expr="pokes + 1"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="bounded" target="bounded">
            <raise event="link"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="spin" target="spin">
            <raise event="link"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="resume" target="resuming">
            <raise event="beat"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="alternate" target="alt">
            <raise event="tick"/>
        </transition>
    </state>

    <!-- A chain that ends on its own. The first transition raises the next
         link while `laps < 999`; at nine hundred and ninety-nine the second
         one answers instead, takes the thousandth microstep and raises
         nothing, so the queue empties and the macrostep completes the way
         §scxml-3.13 describes. An engine must count nothing for it: a long
         chain is not a runaway, and a document that computes for a thousand
         microsteps before settling is an ordinary document.

         Two transitions rather than an `<if>` so the choice is document-order
         selection, which every backend resolves the same way. -->
    <state id="bounded">
        <transition event="link" cond="laps &lt; 999">
            <assign location="laps" expr="laps + 1"/>
            <raise event="link"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="link">
            <assign location="laps" expr="laps + 1"/>
        </transition>
        <!-- Targetless on purpose: a self transition would re-run
             `<onentry>`, and the driver would be measuring its own probe. -->
        <transition event="poke">
            <assign location="pokes" expr="pokes + 1"/>
        </transition>
    </state>

    <!-- A chain that cannot end. Every link answers the event by raising it
         again, so the internal queue is never empty and §scxml-3.13's
         macrostep never reaches its stable configuration. This is the document
         Appendix D says is allowed to run forever; the engine is the only
         party that can decide otherwise, and the count is how it says that it
         did. -->
    <state id="spin">
        <transition event="link">
            <assign location="links" expr="links + 1"/>
            <raise event="link"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="poke">
            <assign location="pokes" expr="pokes + 1"/>
        </transition>
    </state>

    <!-- A chain half again as long as the ceiling: fifteen hundred links, so
         the first macrostep takes a thousand of them and is refused, and the
         second takes the remaining five hundred and ends on its own. What it
         measures is what the refusal did with the links it would not run — an
         engine that dropped them ends at a thousand and never finishes, and
         one that ran them anyway ends the whole chain in the first macrostep.
         Neither of those can be told from the correct answer by any other
         outcome here. -->
    <state id="resuming">
        <transition event="beat" cond="beats &lt; 1499">
            <assign location="beats" expr="beats + 1"/>
            <raise event="beat"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="beat">
            <assign location="beats" expr="beats + 1"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="poke">
            <assign location="pokes" expr="pokes + 1"/>
        </transition>
    </state>

    <!-- A chain that alternates the two branches of Appendix D's inner loop.
         The internal half consumes `tick` and hands the baton to the eventless
         half; the eventless half takes it back and raises the next `tick`.
         Neither branch ever takes two microsteps in a row, so neither reaches
         a hundred on its own — and an engine that budgets them separately runs
         this document forever while both of its ceilings sit half spent. -->
    <state id="alt">
        <transition event="tick">
            <assign location="alts" expr="alts + 1"/>
            <assign location="pending" expr="1"/>
        </transition>
        <transition cond="pending == 1">
            <assign location="pending" expr="0"/>
            <raise event="tick"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="poke">
            <assign location="pokes" expr="pokes + 1"/>
        </transition>
    </state>
</scxml>
